<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>
  <!--
    The trait the armor grants on equip (its ApparelExtension.traitsOnEquip) in place of
    VFEP_WarcasketTrait: VFEP's trait blocks Intellectual, which the suit leaves its pilot, and
    a child apparel extension can only add to what a granted trait blocks, never subtract. The
    Medic and Overlord repos' traits are the precedent.

    Mirrored at startup, do not tune here. WorkProfile.Mirror
    (Source/1.6/Traits/WorkProfile.cs, run first in Startup.Run on every play-data load)
    overwrites this def from VFEP_WarcasketTrait as the full mod list leaves it, so the mods
    that reshape VFEP's work profile reshape the pilot's without knowing we exist. The
    contract:
      - Every work type VFEP's trait blocks is a checkbox in the mod settings. The Intellectual
        ones (Research, Dark study under Anomaly, a modded one) default to open, the rest to
        blocked.
      - disabledWorkTags = VFEP's, with ManualDumb swapped for Cleaning (VFEP's hauling
        exemption is a Harmony postfix keyed on its own def, so a copied ManualDumb would lose
        hauling; ManualDumb covers only Hauling and Cleaning) and every tag an open work type
        carries removed.
      - disabledWorkTypes = each blocked work type that the remaining tags no longer cover.
      - degreeDatas[0]: every public field but the text, degree and commonality copied from
        VFEP's degree, and the TraitExtension's fields copied from VFEP's extension. Both
        copies are full overwrites, nulls included, so the pilot carries exactly what VFEP's
        trait carries on the running game version: on 1.7, painFactor 0 and RestFallRateFactor
        1.3 on the degree; on 1.6, painFactor 0 on the extension and no stat factors.
    The values below are the mirror's result from an unpatched VFEP trait on 1.7 under the
    default settings, so the def reads correctly on its own. Every field used exists on 1.6 as
    well.

    The extension omits painFactor on purpose, as VFEP's 1.7 copy does: VEF's TraitExtension
    leaves the float uninitialised and its CalculatePain postfix multiplies pain by any value
    other than 1, so an omitted painFactor is zero pain. apparelExclusiveTrait keeps
    trait-adding mods and the trait-rolling code paths from handing this trait to a pawn
    outside the suit (VEF's TraitSet.GainTrait prefix admits it only while a worn apparel lists
    it in traitsOnEquip).

    The label is VFEP's own, so the Bio tab names the state as it does for every warcasket
    pilot. The description is VFEP's trait text with only the work clause changed. That clause
    names the work the suit is built to leave open, not the live profile, which the settings
    and patch mods can widen or narrow; the tooltip lists the actual work disables beneath it.
  -->
  <TraitDef>
    <defName>HPWC_WarcasketTrait_Hemophage</defName>
    <commonality>0</commonality>
    <disabledWorkTags>
      <li>ManualSkilled</li>
      <li>Cleaning</li>
      <li>Animals</li>
      <li>Artistic</li>
      <li>Caring</li>
    </disabledWorkTags>
    <degreeDatas>
      <li>
        <label>warcasket</label>
        <description>{PAWN_nameDef} is entombed in a warcasket. No amount of pain will have an effect on {PAWN_objective}, and {PAWN_pronoun} can never go into a pain shock or drop unconscious due to pain. Because of how bulky the armor is and how much energy it takes to use it, {PAWN_nameDef} is not capable of most work, but can still do intellectual work.</description>
        <painFactor>0</painFactor>
        <statFactors>
          <RestFallRateFactor>1.3</RestFallRateFactor>
        </statFactors>
      </li>
    </degreeDatas>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="VEF.Pawns.TraitExtension">
        <apparelExclusiveTrait>true</apparelExclusiveTrait>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </TraitDef>
</Defs>
